<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For years I ran a law practice, and the hardest part of client work was never the work. It was keeping track of it. Where that email went, what we agreed to on a phone call, which version of the document was current, who I owed a call back. Some of it lived in Mail, some in a folder somewhere, and too much of it lived only in my head.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That&#8217;s the problem <a href="https://www.daylite.app?utm_source=macsparky&amp;utm_medium=blog&amp;utm_campaign=crm">Daylite</a> has been chipping away at for more than twenty years. It pulls your clients, projects, tasks, calendars, and communications into one place, so every email, meeting, note, and follow-up becomes part of an ongoing history of that relationship. Open up a client, and you can see what happened, what&#8217;s next, and who is waiting on you.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The newest piece of that is Mail in Daylite, which brings your email inside the app alongside everything else. A message stops being one more thing you have to file. It attaches itself to the person, the deal, and the project it belongs to.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I also like that Daylite is built only for Mac, iPhone, and iPad. It isn&#8217;t a web app wearing a Mac costume, and you can feel the difference in how it behaves.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you run a service business and you&#8217;re tired of carrying the whole client history around in your head, <a href="https://www.daylite.app?utm_source=macsparky&amp;utm_medium=blog&amp;utm_campaign=crm">take a look at Daylite</a>. There&#8217;s a free trial.</p>
